<section xml:id="topic-3-1-lists">
  <time minutes="135" />
  <title>Lists</title>

  <idx>lists</idx>
  <introduction>
    <p>Quick Links:
      <xref text="global" ref="storing-values-in-lists,unplugged-lists,append,changing-lists,list-loops,data-abstraction-with-lists, LOs-3-1, vocab-3-1"/>
    </p>
    <p>
      We use lists every day to keep track of things, for example, shopping lists, to-do lists, 
      or playlists. Python makes it easy to work with lists. In this topic, we will learn to use lists 
      to store values, manipulate list contents, and traverse lists with loops.
    </p>

    <image source="_static/Unit3images/lists.jpg" width="45%" alt="shopping lists"/>

  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="storing-values-in-lists">
    <title>Storing Values in Lists</title>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.A.1</title>
      <p>
        A <term>data collection</term> is a way to store multiple related values together so a program
        can treat them as one unit, such as storing multiple names, scores, or messages.
        Different programming languages support different kinds of collections, such as
        lists, arrays, or dictionaries.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.A.2</title>
      <p>
        Data collections help programs support user-facing features that involve many items,
        such as a list of saved favorites, recent searches, or a playlist. Storing related
        values in one variable makes it easier to manage, update, and process the information
        as users interact with an app.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>All programming languages have ways to store collections of data. Python has a built-in data 
    type called a <term>list</term> which can store an ordered sequence of items. 
    Lists are sometimes called arrays in other programming languages. Here are some examples 
    of lists in Python:</p>
    <program language="python">
        <code>
# A shopping list
shopping_list = ["milk", "eggs", "bread", "butter"]
# A list of scores
scores = [95, 87, 92, 100]
        </code>
    </program>
    
   <note>
      <title>3.1.A.3</title>
      <p>
        A value stored inside a data collection is an <term>element</term>. Programs can work with 
        the entire collection of items as one unit or with individual elements.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.A.4</title>
      <p>
        In a <term>list</term>, elements are stored in an ordered sequence, meaning the position of each
        element matters (first, second, third, etc.). When the following code runs, a list
        called <c>aList</c> contains the elements <c>v1, v2, v3</c>, and so on.
      </p>
      <pre>
aList = [v1, v2, v3, ...]</pre>
    </note>

<idx>index</idx>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.A.7</title>
      <p>
        An <term>index</term> is a number used to reference a specific element by position in a list or
        in a string. Many programming languages use zero-based indexing, meaning the first
        element is at index 0, the second at index 1, and so on.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>
     You can imagine a list as a row of lockers where each locker holds one item or <term>element</term>. 
    The lockers are numbered with an <term>index</term> that starts at 0 for the first locker, 1 for the second locker, and so on.
    Most programming languages, including Python, use zero-based indexing where the first item in 
    the list is at index 0. Just like binary code, computers almost always start counting at 0. 
    </p>

    <image source="_static/Unit3images/lockers.jpg" width="70%" alt="a row of numbered lockers"/>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.A.8</title>
      <p>
        Programs can access a specific list element using its index. This allows programs to
        read or use stored values in a list. When the following code runs, the element in
        <c>aList</c> at index <c>j</c> is accessed.
      </p>
      <pre>
aList[j]</pre>
    </note>

    <p>Imagine a Python list called <c>lockers</c>. Let's put your books in locker 0 and 
        a backpack in locker 1 with the code below. To get to a specific locker or a specific 
        <term>element</term> in a list, use the list name followed by square brackets and the 
        <term>index</term> (number) of the element you want to access like below.</p>
    <program language="python">
        <code>
lockers[0] = "books"
lockers[1] = "backpack"
print("What's in locker 0?", lockers[0] )
print("What's in locker 1?", lockers[1] )
        </code>
        </program>

        <p>Let's try it below:</p>

<activity label="lockers-index">
    <statement><p>Using the <c>lockers</c> list below, print what is in locker 0 and locker 2
    using the list name and the index in square brackets, for example: <c>lockers[1]</c>.</p></statement>
     <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
lockers = ["books", "backpack", "jacket", "lunchbox"]
# print what is in the first locker and the last locker
# using the list name and the index in square brackets

        </code>
        <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        self.assertIn("lockers[0]", self.getEditorText(), "Use lockers[0]")
        self.assertIn("lockers[3]", self.getEditorText(), "Use lockers[3]")
        self.assertIn("books", self.getOutput(), "Print books")
        self.assertIn("lunchbox", self.getOutput(), "Print lunchbox")

myTests().main()
        </tests>
        </program>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="unplugged-lists">
    <title>Unplugged Activity: List of Chairs</title>
  
    <idx><h>unplugged</h><h>ifs</h></idx>
    <idx><h>projects</h><h>unplugged list of chairs</h></idx>

     <image source="_static/Unit3images/chairs.jpg" width="70%" alt="a row of numbered chairs"/>

     <project label="unplugged-list-of-chairs">
       <title>List of Chairs</title>
      <statement>
    <p>
        In groups or as a class, simulate a list using students in chairs.
        Organize a line of empty chairs as the list. 
        Each student who sits in a chair represents an element in the list.
        The first chair in the line has the index 0. The second chair has the index 1, and so on.
        Have students follow the code below to swap students in chairs, for example,
        <c>chairs[0] = None</c> means that the student in chair 0 gets up and empties the chair, 
        and <c>chairs[0] = chairs[1]</c> means that the student in chair 1 moves to chair 0.
        Substitute in the names of students in your class.
      </p>
        <program language="python">
            <code>
# Fill the chairs with students
chairs = ["Alex", "Sam", "Ryan"]
# Example code to swap students in chairs
chairs[0] = None
chairs[0] = chairs[1]
chairs[1] = None
chairs[1] = chairs[2]
chairs[2] = "Alex" # substitute the name of the student who used to be in chair 0
            </code>
        </program>
       <p>    
       What did the code above do? Write your own code below to rotate students the other way
       and then act it out.  
      </p></statement>

       <response/>
     </project>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="append">
    <title>Appending to Lists</title>
 <idx>append</idx>
 <idx>empty list</idx>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.A.5</title>
      <p>
        Lists can start empty, which allows a program to collect information, such as items
        a user adds, results found during a search, or responses entered in a form. When the
        following code runs, an empty list is assigned to <c>aList</c>.
      </p>
      <pre>
aList = [ ]</pre>
    </note>

    <p>If you do not know what values you want to store in a list when you create it, 
        you can start with <term>empty list ([])</term> which is a list that contains no elements and
        add in elements while the program is running. For example, we can start with an empty shopping list.</p>
    <program language="python">
        <code>
# An empty shopping list
shopping_list = []
        </code>
</program>
    <note>
      <title>3.1.A.6</title>
      <p>
        Lists can be built over time by adding new elements, often as the user enters
        information or the program collects it. When the following code runs, <c>value</c>
        is added to the end of <c>aList</c> and the length of <c>aList</c> is increased by 1.
      </p>
      <pre>
aList.append(value)</pre>
    </note>

    <p>Elements can be added to a list using the <c>append</c> method which adds the element 
        to the end of the list.</p>
    <program language="python">
        <code>
# An empty shopping list
shopping_list = []
shopping_list.append("milk")
shopping_list.append("eggs")
shopping_list.append("bread")
print(shopping_list)
        </code>
        </program>

    <idx>
      <h>list</h><h>length</h>
    </idx>
    <idx>
      <h>list</h><h>len</h>
    </idx>

      <note>
      <title>3.1.B.3</title>
      <p>
        The length of a list can change while a program runs as elements are added or
        removed. Knowing the length of a list helps programmers write code that works
        correctly as the list changes. When the following code runs, it evaluates to
        the number of items in <c>aList</c>.
      </p>
      <pre>
length(aList)</pre>
    </note>
        
    <p>The length of the list will increase as elements are added. 
    In Python, you can get the length of a list by using the <c>len(list)</c> function, note the
    shortened form of len for length.
    Let's try <c>append</c> and <c>len</c> below.</p>

    <activity label="append-to-list">
    <statement><p>Add two more items to the <c>shopping_list</c> below.
    </p></statement>
        <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
            <code>
shopping_list = []
shopping_list.append("milk")
shopping_list.append("eggs")
shopping_list.append("bread")
print("Length of shopping list: ", len(shopping_list))
print(shopping_list)
# Add 2 more elements to the shopping_list and then print it!

</code>
            <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        self.assertGreaterEqual(5, len(shopping_list), "shopping_list should have at least 5 elements")
        count = self.getEditorText().count("append")
        self.assertGreaterEqual(count, 5, "at least 5 appends used")

myTests().main()
            </tests>
        </program>
    </activity>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.A.9</title>
      <p>
        Lists support apps that respond to users by remembering and reusing information.
        For example, a music app can store a user's queued songs in a list. Each time the
        user taps "Add to Queue," the song title is appended to the list, and the app uses
        the list to display the upcoming songs in order and play the next track.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="changing-lists">
    <title>Changing List Elements</title>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.B.2</title>
      <p>
        Elements in a list can be changed to different values, which allows a program to
        update stored information without rebuilding the entire list. When the following
        code runs, the value of <c>x</c> is assigned to the element at index <c>j</c> in
        <c>aList</c>. The previous value at that index is overwritten, and the length of
        the list does not change.
      </p>
      <pre>
aList[j] = x</pre>
    </note>

        <note>
      <title>3.1.B.1</title>
      <p>
        Programmers sometimes make a copy of a list so they can modify the copy, such as
        removing or reordering elements, while keeping the original list unchanged.
        Preserving the original list can make a program easier to understand, test, and
        debug because the program can still refer back to the original data if needed.
        When the following code runs, a copy of the list <c>bList</c> is assigned to the
        list <c>aList</c>.
      </p>
      <pre>
aList = bList</pre>
    </note>

    <p>Before making changes to a list, it's often a good idea to make a copy of it first. 
        Then the original list can be preserved and used later if needed. The <c>[index]</c>
        notation can be used to access and change elements in a list.  
    </p>
    <program language="python">
        <code>
# A list of scores
scores = [95, 87, 92, 100]
# Make a copy of the scores list
scores_bonus = scores
# Add bonus points to the first two scores in the copy
scores_bonus[0] += 5
scores_bonus[1] += 5
</code>
</program>




        <idx>insert</idx>
    <note>
      <title>3.1.B.4</title>
      <p>
        Elements can be inserted at particular positions in a list, which allows a program
        to keep items in a meaningful order, such as priority or ranking, as new information
        is added. When the following code runs, any elements at indices greater than or
        equal to <c>j</c> are shifted so that their indices are increased by 1 and
        <c>value</c> is placed at index <c>j</c>. The length of the list is increased by 1.
      </p>
      <pre>
aList.insert(j, value)</pre>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.B.5</title>
      <p>
        Elements at particular positions can be removed from a list, which allows a program
        to delete outdated, incorrect, or unwanted information while keeping the remaining
        elements usable. When the following code runs, the element at index <c>j</c> is
        removed and the elements after it shift one position so that their indices are
        decreased by 1. The length of the list is decreased by 1.
      </p>
      <pre>
aList.remove(j)</pre>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.B.6</title>
      <p>
        To select a random element from a list, a program can generate a random index and
        use it to access an element. This allows for features like selecting a random song
        from a playlist, choosing a random prompt in a game, or assigning a random partner
        from a list of names. In programming languages that use zero-based indexing, if
        <c>aList</c> has <c>length(aList)</c> items, the valid indices are 0 through
        <c>length(aList) - 1</c>. When the following code runs, the program generates one
        of the valid indices with equal likelihood and accesses the element at that index.
      </p>
      <pre>
aList[random(0, length(aList) - 1)]</pre>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.B.7</title>
      <p>
        Apps often update lists in response to user actions. Adding or removing items
        supports user goals such as saving favorites, managing a to-do list, updating a
        roster, or keeping a recent-search history. Programmers choose list operations
        that match how users expect information to change as they interact with the app.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.B.8</title>
      <p>
        To determine what output a program produces after list operations, programmers
        trace statements in order and update the list after each step. This includes
        tracking how element positions change when items are inserted or removed and using
        the updated list to determine what values are accessed or displayed next.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.B.9</title>
      <p>
        List operations that use an index depend on the index being valid for the current
        list. When a program accesses, updates, inserts, or removes using an index,
        programmers must consider the current length of the list and how earlier operations
        may have shifted element positions. In many programming languages, using an index
        that is outside the allowed range produces an error and may cause the program to stop.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="list-loops">
    <title>Traversing Lists with Loops</title>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.C.1</title>
      <p>
        Traversing a list is often used to accomplish a goal, such as counting elements
        that meet a condition, finding a value (like a maximum), computing a total or
        average, or building an output (such as a message that includes all elements that
        match a rule).
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.C.2</title>
      <p>
        Traversals often update an accumulator variable, such as a count or total, each
        time an element is processed.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.C.3</title>
      <p>
        Iteration statements can be used to traverse a list, which allows a program to
        process each element in order without writing repeated code for every element.
        When the following code runs, the variable <c>item</c> is assigned the value of
        each element of <c>aList</c> sequentially, in order, from the first element to
        the last element. The code in block of statements is executed once for each
        element in <c>aList</c>.
      </p>
      <pre>
for item in aList
{
   &lt;block of statements&gt;
}</pre>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.C.4</title>
      <p>
        A linear or sequential search checks elements in order until the desired value is
        found or the list ends. Programs often store the index where a match was found or
        store a Boolean value to indicate whether a match was found.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.C.5</title>
      <p>
        Traversing a list can be a complete traversal, where every element is accessed,
        or a partial traversal, where only some elements are examined. Some partial
        traversals stop early when a goal is reached, such as finding the first match
        in a search, which may require fewer steps than traversing the entire list.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.C.6</title>
      <p>
        Traversing a list supports user-facing features like calculating a total in a
        shopping cart, counting votes in a poll, or checking whether a username is already
        taken. Programmers choose complete vs. partial traversal based on whether a user
        needs to check every element or find a particular match in the list.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.C.7</title>
      <p>
        To determine the result of a traversal, programmers trace the loop and track how
        variables update each time an element is processed.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="data-abstraction-with-lists">
    <title>Data Abstraction with Lists</title>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.D.1</title>
      <p>
        Data abstraction manages complexity by allowing programmers to use data based on
        its name and purpose without needing to focus on how the data is represented.
        This separation helps programmers reason about what the data represents and what
        operations can be done with it.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.D.2</title>
      <p>
        A list is a type of data abstraction because it allows many related values to be
        treated as a single variable. Instead of writing code for many individual
        variables, programmers can use the list's name to perform the same kinds of
        operations on its elements, such as accessing, updating, inserting, removing,
        or traversing.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.D.3</title>
      <p>
        Using data abstractions such as lists can make programs easier to develop, read,
        test, and maintain because the same algorithm can often work even when the amount
        of data changes. When elements are added or removed, the code structure often
        stays the same.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.D.4</title>
      <p>
        Data abstraction makes it easier to adapt a program as user needs change (for
        example, handling more saved favorites, more songs on a playlist, or more
        messages) without redesigning the entire program.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.1.D.5</title>
      <p>
        Data abstraction reduces complexity by reducing duplication in code. For example,
        using a single list and a loop can process any number of elements, instead of
        requiring many separate variables and repeated statements. This means fewer places
        to update code during development, which lowers the chance of errors.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="LOs-3-1">
    <title>Learning Objectives Review</title>

    <remark>
      <title>3.1.A</title>
      <p>
        Develop code that stores multiple related values in a single variable.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>3.1.B</title>
      <p>
        Develop code to manipulate a list, and determine the result produced.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>3.1.C</title>
      <p>
        Develop code using iteration statements to traverse a list, and determine the result produced.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>3.1.D</title>
      <p>
        Explain how the use of data abstraction manages complexity in program code.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

